## 2. marker mode - the default (profiles a kernel)

### What it does

QEMU1's plugin pattern-matches the encoding `slti x0, x0, 0x5A0` at retire
time. On hit it drains state to `outfile` and exits 200. vsim does the
same on `slti x0, x0, 0x5A1`. The markers are RISC-V HINT NOPs (rd=x0), safe on every
core, so the fixture binary embeds these two `slti` instructions at the
kernel boundaries. vsim's drain at the `slti x0, x0, 0x5A1` marker is the
natural tail of the run.

### Inputs

- Fixture ELF with `slti x0, x0, 0x5A0` and `slti x0, x0, 0x5A1` in its source.
  Every C-level fixture (rt_c_v_matmul, rt_c_v_regs, rt_c_matmul, etc.) has these.

### Cycle figure

Kernel cycles = the vsim `mcycle` delta over the region between the two
markers. Run with `CHECK_MCYCLE_MONOTONIC=1` and the harness prints
`mcycle` at both ends:

```sh
FIXTURE=rt_c_v_matmul TRIGGER_MODE=marker CHECK_MCYCLE_MONOTONIC=1 TIMEOUT=120 \
  bash tests/e2e.sh
```

Look for the two lines `qemu1: mcycle=...` and `vsim: mcycle=...`; the
kernel cycle count is `vsim - qemu1` (the GEMM kernel is ~22808 cycles).

### Run it

```sh
cd /home/nick/work/hybrid_sim
./andes-sim run tests/fixtures/rt_c_v_matmul.elf --mode hybrid --trigger marker --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium
```

`./andes-sim run --mode hybrid --trigger marker` orchestrates the QEMU1 → vsim handoff directly (driver/cmd_run.cpp). Before the final PASS line it prints the cycle figure:

```
[marker window_cycles] 22808  (vsim mcycle=... - qemu mcycle=...)
PASS: --mode hybrid --trigger marker (rt_c_v_matmul)
```

The plugin gets `outfile=<state.bin>` and nothing else - that is the marker
default. vsim is launched without `--hybrid-exit-pc` and without
`--hybrid-icount`, so its ELF pre-scanner watches for `slti x0, x0, 0x5A1` PCs
at retire.

### Exercise 1

The `./andes-sim run` examples default to the GEMM, but the harness is
