## 3. PC mode - same kernel, no source edit

### What it does

Same round-trip as marker mode, but the trigger is a PC value, not an opcode
match. The harness resolves each boundary - taken from the required
`--pc-start` / `--pc-end` args (env-var form: `ROI_START` / `ROI_END`) -
to an address (a `0x` value is used verbatim; a name is looked up with
`nm`, see `tests/lib/roi.sh`), then passes:

- `enter_pc=0x<addr>` to the QEMU plugin (QEMU1 drains there)
- `--hybrid-exit-pc 0x<addr>` to vsim (vsim drains there)

marker-mode matchers are bypassed; the marker instructions become
inert. This proves you can hand off WITHOUT modifying the source: point the
boundaries at any function or address the binary already has.

### Inputs

- Same fixture as marker mode (`rt_c_v_matmul`). Both PC boundaries are
  **always explicit** -- pass `--pc-start S --pc-end E` (each a symbol
  name or `0x` address). There is no implicit fallback to planted
  `_hybrid_*_pc` markers.

### Cycle figure

Identical to marker mode: kernel cycles = vsim `mcycle` - QEMU1 `mcycle`.
The boundaries differ, the measurement does not.

### Run it

Resolve `--pc-end` from the binary first (the Drill-down below shows how);
`0x<post-jal>` is a placeholder for the instruction right after the call
into the kernel:

```sh
./andes-sim run tests/fixtures/rt_c_v_matmul_free.elf --mode hybrid --trigger pc --engine vsim:ax45mpv_premium \
  --pc-start vmatmul_kernel --pc-end 0x<post-jal>   # resolve 0x<post-jal> via the Drill-down
```

The same run through the regression harness (adds the monotonic-cycle
assertion):

```sh
FIXTURE=rt_c_v_matmul TRIGGER_MODE=pc CHECK_MCYCLE_MONOTONIC=1 TIMEOUT=120 \
  ROI_START=vmatmul_kernel ROI_END=0x<post-jal> \
  bash tests/e2e.sh
```

### Drill-down

```sh
NM=$HYBRID_TOOLCHAIN/bin/riscv64-unknown-elf-nm
OBJDUMP=$HYBRID_TOOLCHAIN/bin/riscv64-unknown-elf-objdump
ELF=tests/fixtures/rt_c_v_matmul_free.elf
ENTER_PC=$("$NM" --defined-only "$ELF" | awk '$3=="vmatmul_kernel"{print "0x"$1}')
EXIT_PC=$("$OBJDUMP" -d "$ELF" \
  | awk '/<main>:/{flag=1} flag&&/jal[[:space:]]+.*<vmatmul_kernel>/{getline; print "0x"$1; exit}' \
  | tr -d ':')

# QEMU plugin arg
... -plugin libhybrid_handoff.so,outfile=$STATE,enter_pc=$ENTER_PC

# vsim arg
... --hybrid-exit-pc $EXIT_PC
```

### Exercise 2

Compare exit values: run the same fixture with `TRIGGER_MODE=marker` and again
with `TRIGGER_MODE=pc`. Both must exit 0 and both must drain the same PC:

```sh
xxd state_csr.bin | head -4
xxd state_pc.bin  | head -4
```

The PC field is at offset 16 (`hybrid_state_v1::pc`). For most fixtures
`pc(marker) == pc(pc)` because both modes drain right after the entry trigger
retires - so they also report the same kernel cycle delta.

